    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="17">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 17</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="320" ed="#liebermann"/>[And<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="15" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.15"/> ne gesece nan man ðone] cingc<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.2"/>, butan<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="13" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.13"/> he ne mote beon nanes rihtes wyrðe<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="16" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.16"/> innan his hundrede.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="320" ed="#liebermann"/>Ne geséce nan man ðonne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.3"/> cyng, buton he ne mote beon nanes rihtes wurðe innan his hundrede.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-a"><pb n="320" ed="#liebermann"/>And ne gesece nan man þonne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.1"/> cyningc, buton he ne mote beon nanes rihtes wirðe innan his hundrede.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="321" ed="#liebermann"/>Nemo regem<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.10"/> requirat de iusticia facienda, dum ei<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.11"/> rectum offertur in hundreto<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="12" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.12"/> suo.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-inst-cn"><pb n="321" ed="#liebermann"/>Constituantur popularia placita<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="12" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.12-2"/>, quod Angli uocant hundred<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="13" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.13-2"/>, sicut iustum est et<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="*" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.star"/> ante hoc<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="14" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.14"/> factum est, super forisfacturam quam lex docet; et nemo requirat regem, nisi non<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="15" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.15-2"/> possit habere<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="16" target="#IICn_17.lnote.oe.16-2"/> rectitudinem in hundredo suo.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-cons-cn"><pb n="321" ed="#liebermann"/>Neque adeat quisquis regem, nisi nequeat rectum habere in suo centenario.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="17" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="182" ed="#robertson"/>Ne geséce nan man ðonn<ptr type="editorNote" n="6" target="#IICn_17.robnote.fn.6"/>e cyng, buton he ne mote beon nanes rihtes wurðe innan his hundrede.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="262" ed="#rabin-doml"/>And ne gesece nan man ðone cingc butan he ne mote beon nanes rihtes wyrðe innan his hundrede.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="321" ed="#liebermann"/>Und niemand gehe den König an, ausser wenn er innerhalb seiner Hundertschaft<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_17.lnote.de.1"/> [seines] Rechtes nicht theilhaftig werden kann.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">And let no one apply to the king, unless he cannot obtain [his] rights within his hundred.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b"><ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#IICn_17.robnote.n.1"/><pb n="183" ed="#robertson"/>And no-one shall appeal to the king, unless he fails to obtain justice within his hundred.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="263" ed="#rabin-doml"/>And no one may petition the king unless he cannot obtain his rights within his hundred.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="321"><ref target="clause:IIIEg_2">III Eg 2</ref></note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">Vgl. III Eg. 2</note>
        <note type="comparedBy" source="#liebermann"><ref target="clause:LeisWl_43">Leis Wl 43</ref></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="358"><label>source (roman)</label> <ref target="clause:IIIEg_2">III Eg 2</ref> <quote>Table 5.4, II Cn 17: III Eg 2</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="379"><label>Edgar-derived</label> <ref target="clause:IIIEg_2">III Eg 2</ref> <quote>one of his Edgar-derived clauses positively invited exposition on the latter [n. II Cn 17 – 17:1; cf. III Eg 2, 5]</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="379"><label>Edgar-derived</label> <ref target="clause:IIIEg_5">III Eg 5</ref> <quote>one of his Edgar-derived clauses positively invited exposition on the latter [n. II Cn 17 – 17:1; cf. III Eg 2, 5]</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-ppmel" n="194"><label>based on</label> <ref target="clause:IIIEg_2">III Eg 2</ref> <quote>We find this pattern clearly prescribed in Cnut's laws, based on those of Edgar: 2 a. II Cnut 17 – 19:1 (III Edgar 2, 5 – 5:2, except where in bold)</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-ppmel" n="194"><label>based on</label> <ref target="clause:IIIEg_5">III Eg 5–5.2</ref> <quote>We find this pattern clearly prescribed in Cnut's laws, based on those of Edgar: 2 a. II Cnut 17 – 19:1 (III Edgar 2, 5 – 5:2, except where in bold)</quote></note>
        <note type="editorial">G's ‘[And ne gesece nan man þone]’ is written over an erasure in the 16th century. Liebermann compares III Eg 2.; BR: added from Gesetze I p. 321, October 2026 (MS Cons)</note>
        <note type="textual" source="#rabin-doml">And . . . ðone: written in a sixteenth-century hand over an erasure in G</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">320</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sic</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.10" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">req. reg. <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">req. reg. T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.11" n="11" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt K</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in K</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.12" n="12" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-edo <hi rend="italic">R</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-edo R</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.15" n="15" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">320</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">And — ðone <hi rend="italic">im</hi> 16. <hi rend="italic">Jh. über Rasur G</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">And — ðone in the 16th cent. over an erasure G</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">320</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">cyning <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">cyning Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.13" n="13" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">320</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">buton <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">buton Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.16" n="16" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">320</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">wir. <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">wir. Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">320</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl. o.</hi> 15, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">cf. above 15, 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.12-2" n="12" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">am Rande später Cb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">later in the margin Cb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.13-2" n="13" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-ræd <hi rend="italic">H</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-ræd H</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.star" n="*" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">aus III Eg.</hi> 5? <hi rend="italic">Vgl. o. S.</hi> 197. 199</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">from III Eg. 5? Cf. above pp. 197. 199</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.14" n="14" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">antehac <hi rend="italic">H</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">antehac H</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.15-2" n="15" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Lb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Lb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.oe.16-2" n="16" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Di</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Di</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_17.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">205</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Übersetzt durch Leis Wl 43.—‘zu Haus’ las III Eg, schwerlich andres meinend; wohl auch ein Argument, dass der Name <hi rend="italic">hundred</hi> (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 4) fürs Lokalgericht c. 970 bis 1030 allgemein technisch wurde. Rechtsweigerung (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 5) versteht Hn 34, 6.—Möglicherweise hat Inst daneben Hn com 1. 4 (<hi rend="italic">o</hi>. I 524) im Sinne; Inst wird benutzt in Ps Cn for 9</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Translated by Leis Wl 43.—III Eg read ‘at home’, hardly meaning anything different; probably also an argument that the name <hi rend="italic">hundred</hi> (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 4) became the general technical term for the local court between c. 970 and 1030. Hn 34, 6 understands it as refusal of justice (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 5).—Possibly Inst also has Hn com 1. 4 (<hi rend="italic">above</hi> I 524) in mind; Inst is used in Ps Cn for 9</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_17.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">354</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. III Edg. 2; Leis Wl. 43.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_17.robnote.fn.6" n="6" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">182</biblScope></bibl><label>ðonne</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">ðone</hi> G, Ld.</seg></note>
      </div>
